TCP/IP MODEL

The TCP/IP Model (Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol) is a networking model that explains how data is transmitted over the Internet and other computer networks. It was developed by the U.S. Department of Defense and is the foundation of the modern Internet.

Unlike the OSI Model which has 7 layers, the TCP/IP Model has 4 layers. Each layer has specific responsibilities, and together they enable reliable communication between devices across networks.

The TCP/IP Model focuses more on practical implementation rather than theoretical structure.

Layers of TCP/IP Model

Application Layer
The Application Layer is the topmost layer of the TCP/IP model. It provides services directly to the user and applications. This layer combines the functions of the Application, Presentation, and Session layers of the OSI model.

It allows applications to communicate over the network and defines protocols for specific services.

Functions of Application Layer
Provides user interface to network services
Handles data formatting and communication setup
Supports services like file transfer, email, and web browsing

Common Protocols
HTTP, HTTPS
FTP
SMTP, POP3, IMAP
DNS
Telnet

Transport Layer
The Transport Layer is responsible for end-to-end communication between devices. It ensures that data is delivered correctly and in the proper order.

This layer decides whether data transmission should be reliable or fast.

Functions of Transport Layer
Segmentation of data
Error detection and correction
Flow control
Port number addressing
End-to-end communication

Protocols Used
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
Provides reliable, connection-oriented communication
Ensures data delivery, ordering, and error recovery

UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
Provides fast, connectionless communication
No guarantee of delivery or order
Used in streaming, gaming, and voice applications

Internet Layer
The Internet Layer is responsible for logical addressing and routing of data across different networks. It decides the best path for data to travel from source to destination.

Functions of Internet Layer
Logical addressing using IP addresses
Routing packets between networks
Packet forwarding
Fragmentation of packets

Protocols Used
IP (Internet Protocol)
ICMP
ARP
RARP

Network Access Layer
The Network Access Layer is the lowest layer of the TCP/IP model. It deals with the physical transmission of data over the network.

This layer combines the Data Link and Physical layers of the OSI model.

Functions of Network Access Layer
Framing of data
Physical addressing using MAC addresses
Error detection at the hardware level
Transmission of bits over the physical medium

Examples
Ethernet
Wi-Fi
PPP

Data Flow in TCP/IP Model
When data is sent from one device to another, it passes through all four layers of the TCP/IP model at the sender side and then moves upward through the layers at the receiver side.

Application Layer creates the data
Transport Layer divides data into segments
Internet Layer adds IP address and routes packets
Network Access Layer transmits data physically

Difference Between OSI Model and TCP/IP Model

OSI Model
Has 7 layers
Theoretical and conceptual model
Developed by ISO

TCP/IP Model
Has 4 layers
Practical and implementation-based model
Developed by DARPA

Conclusion
The TCP/IP Model is the backbone of the Internet. It provides a simple and efficient way to understand how data is transmitted across networks. Even though the OSI Model is useful for learning, the TCP/IP Model is widely used in real-world networking systems.
